    "['peer-as [parameter2]', [{'para_map': [], 'trans_command': 'router bgp 100', 'parent_command': ['router bgp [parameter1]', 'neighbor [parameter1] remote-as [parameter2]'], 'root': 'router bgp [parameter1]', 'source': 'llm', 'origin_reponse': \"##\\nrouter bgp 100\\nneighbor 10.1.2.2 remote-as 100\\n##\\n\\nNote: While the Juniper command specifies the peer AS (100) at the group level, Cisco requires it to be specified per neighbor. Since the neighbor IP (10.1.2.2) was defined in another Juniper command, I've included it here to make the Cisco configuration complete. The AS number remains the same (100) for both local and remote AS in this case, indicating iBGP peering.\", 'trans_template': 'router bgp 100'}, {'para_map': [], 'trans_command': 'neighbor 10.1.2.2 remote-as 100', 'parent_command': ['router bgp [parameter1]', 'neighbor [parameter1] remote-as [parameter2]'], 'root': 'router bgp [parameter1]', 'source': 'llm', 'origin_reponse': \"##\\nrouter bgp 100\\nneighbor 10.1.2.2 remote-as 100\\n##\\n\\nNote: While the Juniper command specifies the peer AS (100) at the group level, Cisco requires it to be specified per neighbor. Since the neighbor IP (10.1.2.2) was defined in another Juniper command, I've included it here to make the Cisco configuration complete. The AS number remains the same (100) for both local and remote AS in this case, indicating iBGP peering.\", 'trans_template': 'neighbor 10.1.2.2 remote-as 100'}]]": 1,
